 "lotr": {
  "title": "LOTR: one gateway, far fewer tokens, and a wall between reading and acting",
  "intro": "LOTR is the gateway gt puts between Claude and the systems you work in: GitHub, Jira, Microsoft 365 (mail, calendar, files, Teams) and any other API or MCP server you register. Instead of plugging each system straight into Claude, you connect them to LOTR once, and Claude reaches all of them through it.",
  "savings": {
   "title": "Why it sips tokens",
   "paragraphs": [
    "Connected directly, every system pours its raw answers into your conversation: one gateway answer can be about 6,000 tokens, and a sweep (\"my open issues\", \"today's unread mail\") makes several calls. Those tokens stay in the conversation and are paid for again on every later turn.",
    "Through LOTR, a helper agent makes those calls in its own separate context and hands your conversation back only the short list you asked for: keys, titles, statuses. Claude also sees LOTR's four tools instead of every connected system's whole tool list, and finds the one operation it needs by searching."
   ],
   "measured_label": "Measured",
   "measured": {
    "direct": 382400,
    "lotr": 8545,
    "note": "25 Jira queries; measured 2026-10-05"
   }
  },
  "safety": {
   "title": "Why it is safer",
   "items": [
    "Every operation is rated by the gateway as a read, a write, or a consent action (sending mail, merging, deleting). Consent actions always ask you first.",
    "Reading and acting are split on purpose: no one agent holds private data, untrusted text and a way to act. Reader agents can only read, whatever the text they read tells them; writer agents get only the one change composed from your own words. A planted instruction in an issue, an email or a web page cannot make Claude send, merge or delete anything.",
    "Credentials never enter the conversation: a connection holds only a reference to where its token is kept (the keychain, gt's sealed store, an owner-only file), never the token. An error names the reference, never the value.",
    "It joins the rest of gt's security: with gt unlock on, a connection needs your fingerprint before an assistant can use it; with paranoid's action tokens, each LOTR read, write or consent can require a single-use permit you create."
   ]
  },
  "connect": {
   "title": "Connecting a system",
   "paragraphs": [
    "GitHub, Jira and Microsoft 365 have two routes. The built-in REST connection (lotr add-http with --profile github, jira-v3, jira-v2 or graph) works with the specialist agents and recipes. The vendor's own hosted MCP server is a catalog template: fetch the catalog, then set up the one you want (atlassian for Jira, github, or m365).",
    "Every other catalog template is an MCP server too, set up the same way. The setup checks the template, stores the credential, adds the connection and tries it once."
   ],
   "type": [
    "lotr template update",
    "lotr setup atlassian",
    "lotr setup github",
    "lotr setup m365"
   ],
   "catalog": [
    {
     "how": "Hosted, browser sign-in",
     "names": "linear, sentry, notion, atlassian, gitlab"
    },
    {
     "how": "Hosted, your own OAuth app",
     "names": "pagerduty, google-drive, slack, m365 (Work IQ)"
    },
    {
     "how": "Hosted, with a token",
     "names": "github"
    },
    {
     "how": "Local (stdio)",
     "names": "playwright, filesystem, git, fetch, sqlite, postgres"
    }
   ],
   "research": "A server that is not in the catalog: /gt-lotr:gt-lotr-research looks up how to connect it and writes a ready-to-use template and a sourced fact sheet."
  },
  "start": "Set it up and add your first connection: /gt-lotr:gt-lotr. Its page under All commands shows it running.",
  "tests": {
   "tests": 1817,
   "adversarial": 250,
   "invalid": 347
  }
 },
